Project attribution does not require using the original operator's bot,
credentials, support service, channel or infrastructure.

No trademark ownership or right to impersonate the original project is granted.

## 4. Royalty for each completed trade

For each successfully completed trade facilitated by an independent Operator,
the Operator owes the original project:

royalty_sats = original_amount_sats // 1000

This is 0.1% of the original Bitcoin trade amount, rounded down to whole
satoshis. The original amount is the agreed trade principal before operator
fees, not the escrow total including fees or the fiat amount.

The fixed beneficiary is:

bitcoinisfreedombot@coinos.io

Each completed trade creates its own obligation and must have its own
separate payment. Do not batch or aggregate payments for multiple trades.
An obligation that rounds to zero requires no payment.

The royalty is paid from the Operator's commission. It must not be added
as a separate charge to the buyer or seller. Waiving or reducing the
Operator's commission does not waive the royalty; the Operator must fund
any shortfall. Network or wallet payment costs are borne by the Operator
and do not reduce the royalty amount.

Cancelled, failed and refunded trades do not generate completed-trade
royalties. A trade must not be relabelled or its original amount manipulated
to conceal successful completion. Do not charge the same trade twice.
If a previously completed trade is later fully refunded, cancel its unpaid
royalty obligation. If its royalty was already paid, contact Paul to resolve
that specific payment; do not silently offset unrelated trades.

The original project's own deployment is not an independent Operator for
the purpose of remitting a royalty to itself.

## 5. Payment, failures and evidence

Successful payments are recorded locally. No email or notification to Paul is
required for each successful payment. Contact is required for payment faults,
delays or unresolved outcomes as described below; this license does not require
automatic email transmission or collection of user data.

Payment is due immediately after successful completion of each trade.
Operators must configure and maintain a suitable Lightning wallet and a
working payment and verification method. The wallet need not be Coinos.

Operators must retain the supplied royalty accounting, payment and verification
functions, or replace them with an equivalent functioning implementation that
preserves these conditions. Disabling collection, changing the beneficiary,
falsifying settlement or bypassing enforcement to evade payment is prohibited.
This clause does not represent a claim that the current development code
already supplies a complete functioning payment integration.

If a technical fault prevents immediate payment, the obligation remains due.
The Operator must notify Paul without undue delay at paulmeloux@proton.me,
describe the fault, and resolve the payment within 72 hours of the trade's
completion. Notification does not extend the deadline or erase the debt.

If any positive royalty obligation remains unpaid or unresolved after that
deadline, the Operator must stop creating and accepting new trades until
the overdue obligations are resolved. Existing trades, refunds, disputes,
recovery and withdrawals necessary to protect users must continue safely.
Do not hold user funds hostage to royalty enforcement.

An accounting entry, request submission or wallet timeout is not proof of
payment. Treat an uncertain payment as potentially successful, reconcile
it before any retry, and never blindly send it again. The deadline does
not justify duplicate payment or a false PAID status. Where reliable
verification is unavailable, keep the payment unresolved and pause new
trades when required.

Retain a minimal trade-to-payment record showing the original amount, royalty,
timestamps, beneficiary, status and verifiable payment reference where available.
Provide relevant redacted evidence to Paul on reasonable request.
Do not send user identities, banking details, credentials, seeds or private keys.
Incident notices must also exclude sensitive user data.

## 6. Breach and termination

Intentional removal of attribution, impersonation, deliberate nonpayment,
beneficiary substitution, payment-record falsification or deliberate
circumvention terminates the violator's permissions under this license
immediately, without notice or a cure period.

For an unintentional material breach not governed by the payment deadline
above, Paul may give written notice requiring correction within 30 days.
Failure to correct it within that period terminates the violator's permissions.

Termination does not erase accrued obligations. It does not terminate the
permissions of other recipients who remain compliant. After termination,
no new trades or redistribution are permitted by the violator. Only the
minimum continued operation necessary to safely settle or refund existing
user obligations is permitted; it is not reinstatement.
Reinstatement after termination requires Paul's written agreement.

